Background and bot speech for the noise handling measurements (brisvox-bench denoise ...).

Eight clips (4 to 8 s, 16 kHz, mono, 16-bit PCM) of eight different speakers, cut from the dev-clean
set of LibriSpeech: Vassil Panayotov, Guoguo Chen, Daniel Povey, Sanjeev Khudanpur, "Librispeech: an ASR
corpus based on public domain audio books", ICASSP 2015, https://www.openslr.org/12/ . Licensed under
Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (CC BY 4.0), https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/ ;
the underlying LibriVox recordings are in the public domain.

Modifications by the brisvox project: one utterance per speaker (the first of 4 to 8 s from the start of the
speaker's directory), decoded from FLAC and converted to 16 kHz mono PCM16 with ffmpeg; nothing else. The
file names carry the speaker and utterance numbers; `clips.json` has each clip's transcript (upper case as in
the corpus), length and the SHA-256 of the original FLAC file.

The clips play the part of a second voice in the caller's line (a television, a person nearby) and of the
bot's own voice leaking back (echo). They are mixed into the speech fixtures at fixed levels by the benchmark,
never committed mixed. No endorsement by the corpus authors or speakers is implied.
